;;; A written fact's `:rule` that names no rule of the program is stored as derived by it —
;;; M1f **W87**, register row `W87/written-rule-no-such-rule`, filed by S1f.7.9 (T1f.7.9.2,
;;; 2026-11-11).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One guest, the fact `(paid Ann :rule zzz)`, and one rule, `seat`. The program declares no
;;; rule `zzz`. No candidate.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Not ruled: it is F24's question. Under (a), a program may not write `:rule`, and under (b),
;;; `:rule` must name a declared rule, the program is refused at load. Under (c) it loads as
;;; written and every surface that names `zzz` says the provenance was not checked.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `91b12be8`, release: the program loads on every command, exit 0. `ein saturate --dump`
;;; prints `(paid Ann :rule zzz)` among the derived facts, `ein render kb` draws it as derived
;;; by `zzz`, and the state file `ein solve --dump-answer` writes says `(paid Ann) ; rule zzz —
;;; Ann has paid`.
;;; Open, and F24's: what a written provenance may claim is the user's question, not ruled.

(relation paid Guest :why "{?1} has paid")
(relation seated Guest :why "{?1} is seated")
(is-a Ann Guest)
(paid Ann :rule zzz)
(rule seat () :match (paid ?g) :assert (seated ?g) :why "{?g} is seated: they have paid")
(solve :hypothesis-relations ())
(query :goal (seated ?g))
